# README - Queue Operations

## Aim
This folder demonstrates basic queue operations using an array and a linked list.

---

## Algorithm
1. Initialize the queue as empty.
2. Enqueue new values at the rear.
3. Dequeue values from the front; the array implementation shifts remaining values forward.
4. Check whether the queue is empty and, in the linked-list implementation, display its values.

---

## How it works
A queue follows the FIFO (First In, First Out) principle. The array implementation stores up to 100 values and shifts all remaining values on dequeue. The linked-list implementation tracks front and rear pointers, adding at the rear and removing from the front.

---

## Functionality
- Enqueue and dequeue values
- Check whether the queue is empty
- Display the queue in the linked-list implementation
- Report full and empty conditions in the array implementation

---

## Time and Space Complexity
- Array enqueue: O(1); dequeue: O(n) due to shifting
- Linked-list enqueue / dequeue: O(1); display: O(n)
- Extra space: O(1) fixed array storage (capacity 100) or O(n) linked-list storage
